// One comparison, and the diagnostic.
//
// The message is idc.py's, word for word, so a program rejected by either
// compiler is rejected with the same explanation -- including where the
// function it collides with was defined, which is the part that makes the
// error actionable.

dup_probe(int i, int j) {
  int found = 0 - 1;
  if((import cfp)[i] == (import cfp)[j]) {
    found = j;
  }
} return int found;
given lz_o (0, 0):(0)
given lz_o (1, 1):(1)

report_dup(int i, int j) {
  note_failure();
  string name_i = fname_at(i);
  dup_report(name_i, j);
} return void;
given lz_o (1, 0):() then check_failed:(1)
given lz_o (0, 1):() then check_failed:(1)

// `cfp` is one entry per `prog` index, and this is where that correspondence
// is cashed in: the index of a fingerprint is used, unchanged, as the index of
// the function it belongs to. The two cases pin the mapping in both
// directions, so anything that makes `cfp` hold a different number of entries
// than `prog` -- per-segment fingerprints, say -- fails here rather than
// silently reporting a collision against the wrong function.
fname_at(int i) {
  string ret_s = s1_of((import prog)[i]);
} return string ret_s;
given lz_o (0):("dup5")
given lz_o (1):("dup9")
